    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/f6369556ac48b69463a86ce3b123176e2db7c1deed972e41d3b9e7a09b918f50.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. $x=\\frac{3}{2}$\nII. $x=\\frac{5}{2}$\nIII. $x=\\frac{7}{2}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Since there is a jump at $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Since there is a break at $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=\\frac{7}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"III only\".\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/4b9383950fe1fc0e90f60c02b812ac1f67d970842ff9ccb403dd925df064a963.png)",
